<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malmedy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malmedy is a town in the province of Liège in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. It grew around the abbey founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remaclus">Saint Remaclus</a> in the 7th century, twin of the abbey at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot">Stavelot</a>, with which it formed an ecclesiastical principality. Given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, it was transferred to Belgium in 1920, and in December 1944 American prisoners were massacred near the town by German troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malmedy.jpg" alt="Malmedy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CathLegrand · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_de_Malmedy_en_mai_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
